Good morning Barbara,

To close out the reconciliation question below: the status history now ties t=
o the year-end census for every year of the study period except the first. We=
 can begin the termination and retirement tabulations on the years that tie, =
and add the first year once one coding question is settled.

For your staff's reference, the reconciliation ran as follows.

1. We took each year-end census and rolled its active count forward to the ne=
xt year-end, using the status changes recorded in the history file.

2. For every year after the first, the rolled-forward count agrees with the f=
ollowing census once the rehires flagged in the file are counted back in as a=
ctives.

3. In the first year, a group of records leaves the active census with no sta=
tus change recorded. Most of the same people reappear that year under a new e=
mployee number, which points to internal transfers rather than terminations. =
The file itself does not say so.

Could someone in your benefits area confirm whether those records are transfe=
rs and, if they are, send the old and new employee number for each person? Un=
til we have that, we will hold them out of the first-year termination counts =
instead of treating them as withdrawals, and the Estes Group data summary wil=
l describe that treatment. Any first-year rate we show before then is prelimi=
nary.

